# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# XD-RobustPVOS -- what is deliberately NOT in this repository.
#
# Rule of thumb: commit code, configs, docs, and the analysis products a
# reviewer needs in order to re-check a number.  Do not commit anything that is
# either (a) large and downloadable, or (b) reproducible from the scripts in
# one run.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# data and weights: never commit  (checkpoints/ alone is ~1.5 GB)
data/
checkpoints/
*.pt
*.pth

# environment
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# results: the two patterns below only match files DIRECTLY under results/,
# because * does not cross a slash.  Nested products therefore survive on
# purpose -- a reviewer can clone and re-check numbers straight away:
#   results/p1/     the P1 mirror every table and figure reads (scripts/115)
#   results/figs/   FIG-1..FIG-5 and the FIGURES.json manifest (124, 128)
results/*.csv
results/*.json
!results/.gitkeep

# A self-test's scratch directory, and the one write that escapes its temp dir:
# `124_fig_build.py --self-test` points its FIG-3 refusal probes at
# <results/figs>/_sevtmp/s.json, because the same controls read the REAL figure
# directory.  Nothing reads it back across runs, so it must not be committed --
# and without this rule a plain `git add .` after a smoke run adds a fresh temp
# file to the release each time.
results/figs/_sevtmp/

# Superseded top-level products, removed 2026-09-29 (checklist 1.15).  Each is
# regenerated by a script that writes to whatever --out it is handed, so a plain
# `05_analysis.py --out results/` or a bare `108_compound_vs_member.py` puts them
# straight back.  Their current versions live under results/p1/ and are what the
# paper reports; these are the pre-P1 run.  Shipping both, one directory apart,
# with neither file stating its convention, is how a reader ends up quoting a
# superseded number.
#
# Listed one by one rather than as `results/*.md` on purpose: two top-level
# artefacts ARE distributed (ablation_table13.md, table11_iqa_correlation.tex),
# and a blanket rule would then need `!` re-negations for them -- which is
# precisely the kind of rule that gets edited wrong later, silently, in the
# direction of publishing more.
results/c1c4_vs_cl4.md
results/c1c4_vs_members.md
results/iqa_config_level.md
results/severity_curve.md
results/table14_stats.tex
results/table9_main.tex
results/xsource.md

# Loose backups of superseded artefacts.  An earlier version of this file let
# them into the release because they do not end in .csv -- which is not a
# criterion, it is an accident of naming.  Removed 2026-09-29.
*.prev

# A root-cause note, withheld for the same reason as the docs below: it records
# why the P1 denominator exists (defect 13) -- which the manuscript already
# states -- and every artefact it cites lives under the unshipped `_scratch/`
# tree, so in a clone its three provenance lines would point at nothing.
# Withheld 2026-09-29; the file itself is kept on disk for local reference.
results/defect13_recharacterisation.md

# Two nested directories that were in the release only by accident, removed
# 2026-09-29.  The two patterns above (`results/*.csv`, `results/*.json`) do not
# cross a slash -- that is deliberate, so that results/p1/ and results/figs/
# survive -- but the same property meant these two survived as well, while their
# top-level twins were excluded.  The result was the wrong way round: the VOIDED
# period's `baselines_raw.csv` shipped while the current one did not.
#
# Neither is evidence.  README already told readers "do not cite" them, but a
# note saying a directory is not evidence is not the same decision as shipping
# it; this is.  No script under scripts/ or xdrp/ reads either path, and the
# 2.8 MB `_superseded/` held the single largest file in the release.
results/_superseded/
results/archive_void_20260916/

# scratch workspaces: sweep temp data plus a downloaded pandoc (~1.6 GB).
# Anchored to the repository root on purpose, so a nested directory that
# happens to share the name is NOT swallowed by this rule.
/_scratch/

# run logs
/logs/

# build artefacts plus submission paperwork -- regenerable via
# scripts/127_make_docx.py, or addressed to the journal rather than to a reader
# of this repository.  Nothing under out/ is needed to re-check a number.
/out/
out/*.docx
out/*.build.json

# loose run logs written to the repository root
_*.txt
_*.log

# The manuscript's source file.  Withheld for a different reason from the notes
# below: the paper is submitted to the journal, and this repository is the code
# plus the evidence behind it.  Its top block is the drafting status table and
# the provenance rule ("every number below is copied from
# docs/PAPER_FRAMEWORK.md") -- scripts/127_make_docx.py strips that block from
# the .docx, so the .md is the only artefact that would expose it.
# It IS declared in scripts/_release_gate.py::UNPUBLISHED, which is what makes
# the five manuscript-side guards report SKIP instead of FAIL in a clone.
/docs/MANUSCRIPT.md

# Internal working notes: the writing plan, the root-cause log, the
# related-work boundary audit and the compute accounting.  These are process
# notes, not research artefacts -- no published number depends on them being
# readable from a clone.  (scripts/118, 121, 122, 123, 125 and 126 read them
# locally; 00_smoke_test asks scripts/_release_gate.py whether they are present
# rather than reading them itself.)
/docs/PAPER_FRAMEWORK.md
/docs/BASELINE_DIAGNOSIS.md
/docs/NOVELTY_BOUNDARY.md
/docs/COMPUTE_UTILIZATION.md
